<!--
SLF4J API (NOT RECOMMENDED IN CURRENT FRAMEWORK SETUP)

Purpose:
- Logging abstraction layer only (does NOT perform logging itself)
- Requires a backend implementation (Logback / Log4j2 / etc.)

Why NOT used here:
- Framework already uses Log4j2 directly (log4j-api + log4j-core)
- Adding SLF4J without standardizing backend creates dual logging pathways
- Increases risk of classpath conflicts and duplicate logs in CI runs

Recommended approach for this framework:
- Either use Log4j2 directly OR
- Use SLF4J + Log4j2 binding (not Logback)
-->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
    <artifactId>slf4j-api</artifactId>
    <version>2.0.12</version>
</dependency>


<!--
LOGBACK CLASSIC (NOT RECOMMENDED IN THIS FRAMEWORK)

Purpose:
- Concrete logging implementation for SLF4J
- Alternative to Log4j2 logging backend

Why NOT used here:
- Conflicts with existing Log4j2 backend
- Leads to multiple logging implementations on classpath
- Can cause unpredictable logging binding at runtime
- Increases debugging complexity in parallel execution environments

Preferred in this framework:
- Log4j2 is the single logging backend (log4j-api + log4j-core)
- Avoid mixing logging frameworks in same runtime
-->
<dependency>
    <groupId>ch.qos.logback</groupId>
    <artifactId>logback-classic</artifactId>
    <version>1.4.14</version>
</dependency>


<!--
	
	If later you migrate to best practice (optional future upgrade)
	
	
SLF4J + Log4j2 Bridge (RECOMMENDED ENTERPRISE SETUP)

Purpose:
- Standard logging abstraction (SLF4J API)
- Delegates actual logging to Log4j2 backend
- Provides flexibility to swap logging implementations in future

Benefits:
- Clean architecture separation (API vs implementation)
- No tight coupling to Log4j2 in business code
- Industry-standard enterprise logging approach
-->

 <!-- ExtentReports (Reporting engine) -->

<dependency>
